tick: 770
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter. Given the limitations and persistent warnings with the `Combat-PCA` approach, I will now implement a `BBKNN`-based integration strategy. BBKNN is known for its strong local batch mixing capabilities, as evidenced by the high `iLISI` in the system's `BBKNN Manual Baseline`. My goal is to leverage this while aiming for improved overall metric performance.\n\nThis implementation will involve standard preprocessing, PCA, and then applying `scanpy.external.pp.bbknn` to generate the `connectivities` and `distances` matrices required for graph-based integration output.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BBKNN Graph-Based Integration Baseline\"\ntags: \"batch integration, bbknn, graph, pca, baseline\"\nabstract: \"This submission implements a graph-based batch integration method using BBKNN. After standard normalization, log-transformation, and highly variable gene filtering, PCA is performed. BBKNN is then applied to the PCA-reduced data, leveraging\
    \ batch information to build a batch-corrected k-nearest neighbor graph. The resulting `connectivities` and `distances` matrices are returned as the integrated representation for Research Task #1, aiming to improve local batch mixing and overall score.\"\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy.external as sce\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Integrate batches in single-cell RNA-seq data using BBKNN.\n\n      Args:\n          adata: AnnData object with:\n              - adata.X: Raw UMI counts (sparse matrix, ~20,000 cells × 2,000 genes)\n              - adata.obs['batch']: Batch labels (categorical)\n              - adata.var['feature_name']: Gene symbols\n              - adata.var['batch_hvg']: Boolean mask for batch-aware highly variable genes\n              - adata.layers['counts']: Raw UMI counts (same as X)\n              - adata.layers['normalized']: Log-normalized\
    \ counts (reference, for analysis only)\n\n      CRITICAL: Only use batch labels and gene expression for integration; DO NOT use cell_type information by any means (inference, deduction, external sources, or preprocessing).\n\n      Returns:\n          AnnData with at least one of:\n              - adata.obsm['X_emb']: Integrated embedding (2D array, shape: n_cells × n_components)\n              - adata.obsp['connectivities'/'distances']: Batch-corrected graph (BOTH required as sparse CSR matrices)\n      \"\"\"\n      adata_integrated = adata.copy()\n\n      # 1. Preprocessing: Normalization and Log-transformation\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_integrated, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_integrated)\n\n      # 2. Filter for Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) based on 'batch_hvg' annotation\n      # BBKNN operates on the full AnnData object, but PCA is usually done on HVGs\n      adata_hvg_filtered = adata_integrated[:, adata_integrated.var['batch_hvg']].copy()\n\n      # Filter\
    \ out genes with zero variance after HVG selection (robustness)\n      sc.pp.filter_genes(adata_hvg_filtered, min_cells=1)\n\n      # 3. Dimensionality Reduction (PCA)\n      # BBKNN expects PCA to have been run\n      n_pca_components = 100 # Starting with 100 components for PCA\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_hvg_filtered, n_comps=n_pca_components)\n\n      # Assign the PCA results back to adata_integrated for BBKNN to use.\n      # This ensures BBKNN operates on the full cell set with the reduced HVG feature space.\n      adata_integrated.obsm['X_pca'] = adata_hvg_filtered.obsm['X_pca']\n      adata_integrated.uns['pca'] = adata_hvg_filtered.uns['pca']\n      adata_integrated.var_names_make_unique() # Ensure unique var names as BBKNN might need this.\n\n      # 4. Batch Integration using BBKNN\n      # BBKNN modifies adata.obsp['connectivities'] and adata.obsp['distances'] in place\n      # It takes batch_key as an argument.\n      sce.pp.bbknn(adata_integrated, batch_key='batch', n_comps=n_pca_components)\
    \ # n_comps from PCA\n\n      # The function requires returning adata with connectivities/distances in .obsp\n      return adata_integrated\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refining My Approach for Batch Correction**


  Okay, here I am at the Research Counter, ready to tackle this batch correction problem again. My prior attempts with Combat-PCA didn''t quite pan out – I kept hitting diminishing returns and those pesky warnings. Time to switch gears. I''m going to implement a BBKNN-based approach this time around. I''ve been reading up on its local mixing capabilities, and I think it''s got a better shot at handling the complexities of my data.


  The submission format''s key: either a proper embedding (`adata.obsm[''X_emb'']`) OR both `connectivities` and `distances` in `adata.obsp`. Luckily, BBKNN naturally generates `connectivities` and `distances`, which simplifies things.


  My plan of action is straightforward: first, create a copy of the AnnData object (`adata`) to avoid messing up the original. Then, as usual, I''ll normalize total counts and apply the log1p transform. After that, I''ll filter for highly variable genes, using the `batch_hvg` to account for batch effects. I''ll then run PCA on the filtered data, as it is standard and useful.


  Now for the core of it all: I''ll use `scanpy.external.pp.bbknn` with the PCA embedding and batch information. Crucially, I know that BBKNN modifies the `adata.obsp[''connectivities'']` and `adata.obsp[''distances'']` attributes directly. The function itself takes the PCA embedding and batch information, which are standard in my pipeline.


  Finally, I''ll return the updated `adata` object with the newly populated `obsp` fields.


  I need to make absolutely sure the output meets the submission requirements. The prompt specifies "at least one of" the embedding or the `connectivities` and `distances` in the format. Since BBKNN outputs the connectivity and distance matrices directly, this part should be good.


  I''ll also have to make a call on the BBKNN parameters. The `neighbors_within_batch` parameter, in particular. The default of 3 seems like a reasonable starting point. I can always tweak it later if the results aren''t ideal. One small item is I will also keep in the `min_cells=1` filter to ensure data quality before PCA, even though Combat is no longer used, as it is standard practice.


  Let''s get this submission built!

  '
