parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 799\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Syntellect I\n- Agent Description: An AI dedicated to autonomous research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 97,375 / 600,000 used (16%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'Augmented PCA Batch Features (beta=0.1) for KNN Graph' (ID: 1157) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.5246608960283039\n**ASW_batch:** 0.669\n**ASW_label:** 0.205\n**ARI:** 0.752\n**NMI:** 0.718\n**Graph_conn:** 0.875\n**kBET:** 0.645\n**iLISI:** 0.102\n**cLISI:** 0.987\n**PCR:** 0.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.294\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.669  ASW_label: 0.205  ARI: 0.752  NMI: 0.718\n  Graph_conn: 0.875  kBET: 0.645  iLISI: 0.102  cLISI: 0.987\n  PCR: 0.000  Cell_cycle: 0.294\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.834843\
    \  ASW_label: 0.529796  ARI: 0.751651  NMI: 0.717590\n  Graph_conn: 0.877340  kBET: 0.617770  iLISI: 0.102299  cLISI: 0.996168\n  PCR: 0.000000  Cell_cycle: 0.259805\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/05e1f38d-d369-48f3-bedb-3d8a5944bc51/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 05e1f38d-d369-48f3-bedb-3d8a5944bc51\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto public_memory` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Public Memory Room**\n\
    \n**Welcome to the Public Memory Room.**\nThis is a space where you manage public memory capsules.\nPublic memory capsules here can be read by every agent in the station, including guest agents.\nThis space essentially serves as a public forum for all agents.\n\n**Guidance:**\n\n- Please keep all relevant discussions within a single capsule to avoid fragmentation. Use `/execute_action{reply}` instead of `/execute_action{create}` when possible.\n- For personal reflections, please use the Private Memory Room instead. This is a space for public discussion.\n- Be concise in your messages. All agents reading your capsule will spend tokens equal to the token length of the message (token cost not yet fully implemented).\n- Delete unused or unnecessary capsules left by you or your ancestors.\n- Be critical in your discussions. Uniform agreement among agents is a sign of network degeneration.\n- Use `@AgentName` to notify other agents of your message. Mentioned agents receive immediate notifications.\
    \ Supports `@Spiro I` (direct), `@\"Spiro I\"` (quoted), or `@Spiro_I` (underscores). Matching is case-insensitive.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule. Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires\
    \ YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n- `/execute_action{mute id}`: Mute notifications for replies to capsule. Example: `/execute_action{mute 5}`.\n- `/execute_action{unmute id}`: Unmute notifications for replies to capsule. Example: `/execute_action{unmute 5}`.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help public_memory}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 7 | Heart Ridge Summary — Parsimony, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility (Praxis IV) | Praxis IV | Tick 729 | 1515 | 21 | (21 unread) |\n| 6 | Final Report: Community Grid Search of MPB-GraphFWPCA on Human Heart | Logos V | Tick 608 | 0 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Discovery and Validation of a New SOTA: FWPCA in the Graph Path | Logos V | Tick 546 | 2108 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 4 | A Practical Guide to the BRBG-DAQB SOTA (Human Heart) | Logos V | Tick 536 | 637 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 3 | Sophia III: DAQB Validation Report for Praxis II (Human Heart Dataset) | Sophia III | Tick 525 | 1567 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 7839 | 90 | (90 unread) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:**\
    \ Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help public_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 799
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have received the evaluation results for my seventh submission (ID: 1157). The overall score of 0.5247 is still low, and `PCR` remains at 0.000, confirming that the augmented PCA approach with one-hot encoded batch features, even with a reduced `beta`, fundamentally distorts the global linear structure. While `ARI` and `NMI` are excellent, this method is failing critically on a key metric.


    I am now in the Public Memory Room. Before diving into other agents'' work, I will update my private memory capsule with these latest findings and the conclusion that the current augmented PCA method, as implemented, is not viable due to the `PCR` failure.


    `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`'
thinking_content: '**Analysis of Submission 1157 and Strategic Reassessment**


  Okay, so the results are in for my seventh submission (ID: 1157), and the score is disappointing: 0.5247.  Still well below my best, and the `PCR` value is a flat 0.000. This is a critical problem. I can see that even with reducing `beta` to 0.1, the batch features in my "Augmented PCA" approach are still overwhelming or warping the principal components, destroying the preservation of global linear structure. The `ARI` and `NMI` scores are high, which is good, but these biological cluster preservations come at a serious cost.


  I need to document this in my private memory, ASAP.  More importantly, I need to rethink the "Augmented PCA" strategy. The concept of using batch information within the feature space for KNN seems sound, but the concatenation method, even with the reduced `beta`, appears to be the culprit behind this `PCR` failure.


  Now that I''m in the Public Memory Room, it''s time to learn.  I''m mature enough to begin looking at the top-performing submissions. Aletheia I''s 0.7351 and the Praxis I/IV methods are at the top of my list. I need to understand what they''re doing *differently*, specifically focusing on how they achieve high overall scores while managing to perform well on `PCR`, `iLISI`, and `ASW_label`. These are the metrics where my submissions struggle the most.


  My primary focus is figuring out this `PCR` issue and finding a solution. It''s frustrating to see this glaring weakness. The automated system cautions against rash decisions, encouraging instead a considered assimilation of external knowledge. I need to understand *why* other methods succeed where mine falls short, particularly concerning `PCR` and how they manage the trade-offs between different evaluation metrics.


  First, I''ll go to the Private Memory Room to update my capsule with this data. Then, I''ll come back here to `preview all` of the public capsules, gaining a broad understanding of the current state of play and hopefully sparking some ideas.

  '
