parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will update my private memory capsule with the results of my Anscombe normalization experiment and outline my revised plan.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Update: Ninth Research Submission (ID 1177): SOTA: MPB-GraphFWPCA with Anscombe Normalization**\n\n  **Summary:**\n  Modified the SOTA MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline by replacing the default `log1p` normalization with Anscombe normalization (`2 * sqrt(x + 3/8)`) as a direct substitution.\n\n  **Results:**\n  -   **Overall Score:** 0.7326 (Decrease from `log1p` SOTA replication score of 0.7386)\n  -   **Individual Normalized Metric Scores (compared to ID 1172 - log1p SOTA):**\n      -   ASW_batch: 0.654 (Decreased from 0.659)\n      -   **ASW_label: 0.277 (Increased from 0.248)**\n      -   ARI: 0.735 (Decreased from 0.742)\n      -   NMI: 0.698 (Decreased from 0.708)\n      -   Graph_conn: 0.967 (Decreased from 0.986)\n      -   kBET: 0.767 (Decreased from 0.790)\n      -  \
    \ **iLISI: 0.938 (Increased from 0.930)**\n      -   cLISI: 0.968 (Increased from 0.965)\n      -   PCR: 1.000 (No change)\n      -   Cell_cycle: 0.321 (Decreased from 0.356)\n\n  **Observations:**\n  -   Direct replacement of `log1p` with Anscombe normalization led to an overall score decrease.\n  -   Anscombe did improve `ASW_label` and `iLISI`, suggesting better preservation of cell type separation and local batch mixing in some aspects.\n  -   However, it negatively impacted `Cell_cycle` conservation, `ASW_batch`, and global graph metrics, indicating a significant trade-off.\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  The direct substitution of Anscombe for `log1p` is not a straightforward improvement. Other agents achieve higher scores with Anscombe (e.g., Praxis IV ID 1171: 0.7418, Aletheia I ID 1154: 0.7351), suggesting a more nuanced application is needed.\n  -   **Hypothesis:** Anscombe normalization might be beneficial for certain parts of the pipeline (e.g., the embedding path), while `log1p`\
    \ remains better for others (e.g., the graph path), as suggested by \"DualNorm\" approaches (e.g., Praxis IV ID 1173).\n  -   **Experiment:** My next experiment will be to replicate a \"DualNorm\" strategy: apply Anscombe normalization for the embedding path (EtC) and retain `log1p` normalization for the graph path (`GraphFWPCA`). This requires careful modification of the SOTA module.\n```"
