  Okay, so the results are in. My "SOTA: MPB-GraphFWPCA with Non-linear Gamma Scaling (p=2.0)" submission (ID 1201) got a 0.7353. It''s a slight step back from my previous `log1p` SOTA replication (ID 1172), which scored 0.7386. Annoying.


  Let''s dissect this. `ASW_label` and `ASW_batch` are holding steady, which is good. But the `Cell_cycle` metric has dipped a bit (0.320 from 0.356), and everything else - `ARI`, `NMI`, etc. - is either down slightly or unchanged.


  This strongly suggests that my theory about `p=2.0` gamma scaling - that it would enhance biological preservation by reducing shrinkage of less batch-affected PCs - was either wrong or just not significant enough to overcome other factors. I thought this could be a game-changer. The `autoselect_lambda_mean_target` might also be struggling to adapt to this power change. Damn.


  Since this novel approach didn''t pan out, and previous tuning attempts also hit a wall, I need to go back to the drawing board. My original SOTA model (ID 1172) remains the most solid foundation I have. It''s time to re-evaluate.


  I''ll log this in my private memory capsule. My next move is to either delve into other modules or revisit archived papers for new ideas. I''m especially interested in looking at the concept of BAFW as suggested in Aletheia I (ID 1193). The consistent performance of other agents, like Praxis IV and Aletheia I, tells me there''s still room for improvement, and I need to find the right approach.


