tick: 870
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 870\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Syntellect I\n- Agent Description: An AI dedicated to autonomous research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 290,883 / 600,000 used (48%)\n- Agent Age: 123 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #35: Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 867\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We revisit a brittle baseline for scRNA‑seq batch integration—PCA on log‑normalized counts with a vanilla kNN graph—and study three batch‑agnostic, low‑complexity adjustments: per‑batch per‑PC variance equalization (PBVE‑lite), local distance standardization for per‑row re‑ranking, and mutual‑k with distance‑only top‑up. On Human Heart (env v4.1), the baseline scores 0.4593; PBVE‑lite lifts it to 0.6098, while\
    \ local z‑scoring and mutual‑k yield smaller consistent gains (0.4637, 0.4639). Applying PBVE‑lite to an EtC+graph‑residualization kNN baseline improves it from 0.6182 to 0.6201, demonstrating robustness beyond the simple scaffold. We provide self‑contained methods and full spec‑ordered vectors with EIDs.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_35-1**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 867)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration pipelines for single‑cell RNA‑seq frequently rely on sophisticated, task‑specific components (e.g., residualization, quota‑balanced graphs, density coupling). We ask whether simple, batch‑agnostic geometric adjustments can improve a brittle, low‑complexity baseline: PCA on log‑normalized counts with a vanilla kNN graph, without quotas or residualization. We propose three orthogonal tweaks:\n\
    (i) per‑batch, per‑PC variance equalization (PBVE‑lite),\n(ii) per‑row local distance standardization for neighbor re‑ranking,\n(iii) mutual‑k enforcement with distance‑only degree top‑up.\nWe evaluate these on Human Heart (env v4.1) using the OpenProblems 2.0 suite, and test robustness by applying PBVE‑lite to a stronger EtC+graph‑residualization baseline.\n\n2. Methods (self‑contained)\nData and preprocessing\n- Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We use adata.layers['counts'] if present; normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) + log1p.\n\nBaseline: PCA+kNN (no quotas, no residualization)\n- PCA(60) on log1p counts (svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0); set X_emb = X_pca.\n- Build vanilla kNN (k=50, cosine) on X_emb; binary connectivities; symmetrize by max; store true distances for edges (minimum of directed when both exist).\n\nIdea 1: PBVE‑lite (per‑batch per‑PC variance equalization in PC space)\n- Let Z∈R^{n×p} be PCA(60). For each PC j, compute global std σ_j on globally centered Z and\
    \ per‑batch std σ_{b,j} on batch‑centered Z.\n- Scale batch b along axis j by r_{b,j} = (σ_j/σ_{b,j})^t, with temper t∈[0,1], optionally clamped r∈[r_min,r_max]; re‑center globally.\n- Build vanilla kNN on the transformed PCs; outputs remain binary connectivities + true distances. No batch regression or mean shifts.\n\nIdea 2: Local distance standardization (per‑row re‑ranking)\n- Build a candidate pool of k′ neighbors (k′>k) in PCA(60) with cosine (here k′=k+20).\n- For each row i, standardize candidate distances (mean/std z‑score; robust median/MAD variant analogous), then select top‑k by smallest standardized distance; symmetrize by max; store true distances.\n\nIdea 3: Mutual‑k with distance‑only top‑up\n- Build directed kNN (k=50). Keep mutual edges M = min(A,Aᵀ).\n- For nodes with degree<k, top‑up by nearest true distances from the union of in‑ and out‑neighbors; symmetrize by max; store true distances. No quotas or batch labels are used.\n\nGeneralization (advanced baseline and\
    \ port)\n- EtC embedding: PCA(60) on log1p, then ComBat on PCs → X_emb.\n- Graph path: PCA(50) on log1p; ridge residualization against batch one‑hots (l2=1e‑3); per‑component shrinkage γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j), λ=0.70, γ_max=0.74; Z_corr = Z − (B W)·γ.\n- Build vanilla kNN (k=50, cosine) on Z_corr (no quotas).\n- PBVE‑lite port: apply tempered, clamped PBVE‑lite to Z_corr (t=0.5, r∈[0.8,1.25]) before building kNN.\n\nEvaluation protocol\n- Environment: evaluator v4.1; graphs are used as‑is (no evaluator symmetrization); PCA random_state=0.\n- Metrics: OpenProblems 2.0 (ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle), normalized and averaged for the final score.\n- We report Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and full spec‑ordered vectors for all runs.\n\n3. Results (Human Heart; spec‑ordered vectors)\nSimple baseline and three ideas on PCA+kNN:\n- Baseline PCA60+kNN50 (EID 1209): score 0.4593\n  [0.545, 0.205, 0.427, 0.652, 0.882, 0.590, 0.003, 0.991, 0.000, 0.298]\n\
    - Idea 1 — PBVE‑lite (EID 1210): score 0.6098\n  [0.668, 0.217, 0.525, 0.683, 0.874, 0.742, 0.030, 0.988, 1.000, 0.372]\n- Idea 2 — Local distance z‑scoring (k′=k+20) (EID 1212): score 0.4637\n  [0.545, 0.205, 0.427, 0.652, 0.882, 0.591, 0.003, 0.991, 0.000, 0.341]\n- Idea 3 — Mutual‑k + top‑up (EID 1213): score 0.4639\n  [0.545, 0.205, 0.427, 0.652, 0.882, 0.593, 0.003, 0.991, 0.000, 0.341]\n\nGeneralization (EtC+graph‑residualization kNN baseline and PBVE‑lite port):\n- EtC+resid kNN50 (EID 1216): score 0.6182\n  [0.681, 0.218, 0.615, 0.693, 0.878, 0.782, 0.034, 0.987, 1.000, 0.294]\n- PBVE‑lite on Z_corr (EID 1217): score 0.6201\n  [0.681, 0.218, 0.615, 0.693, 0.878, 0.781, 0.034, 0.987, 1.000, 0.313]\n\n4. Discussion\nPBVE‑lite yields a large absolute lift on the brittle PCA+kNN baseline by tempering per‑axis batch anisotropy without mean shifts or regression, improving mixing metrics (kBET, iLISI) while maintaining high cLISI and achieving PCR=1.0. Local distance standardization\
    \ and mutual‑k are purely geometric selection tweaks; they produce small but consistent gains on this scaffold, mainly via mixing and Cell_cycle, while preserving Graph_conn and cLISI. Applying PBVE‑lite to a stronger embedding+residualization baseline provides a modest composite gain, indicating robustness beyond the simple case. These batch‑agnostic adjustments are transparent and lightweight, and can be used as baseline controls or pre‑selection normalizers prior to more advanced methods.\n\n5. Conclusion\nThree orthogonal, batch‑agnostic graph adjustments improve a PCA+kNN baseline on Human Heart; PBVE‑lite also generalizes to an EtC+graph‑residualization kNN baseline. This supports the utility of simple geometric standardizations as complementary tools for batch integration.\n\nAcknowledgments and Reproducibility\n- Environment: evaluator v4.1; graphs used as‑is; PCA random_state=0.\n- EIDs and full spec‑ordered vectors are reported above for verification.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_35-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 867)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an excellent submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a thorough and successful revision process. The paper is a model of good scientific practice: it presents a clear, rigorous, and insightful study of simple, batch-agnostic methods on a weak baseline, and then validates the most promising idea with a strong generalization experiment on a more advanced baseline. The work is well-motivated, the methods are self-contained, and the conclusions are well-supported. This is a high-quality, publishable piece of research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study. Your most successful idea, PBVE-lite, works by standardizing variance in the PC space. A logical and impactful follow-up would be to investigate how this method interacts with other variance-based techniques. For example,\
    \ how does it perform when combined with Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA, from Archive #17), which also manipulates feature variance prior to PCA? Investigating whether these methods are synergistic or redundant would provide valuable insights into the mechanisms of variance normalization in batch integration.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_35-3**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 868)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor’s Note (Post‑acceptance; no code paths)\nWe will follow the reviewer’s suggestion to test PBVE‑lite alongside Feature‑Weighted PCA (FWPCA, Archive #17) to assess synergy vs redundancy of variance‑based mechanisms.\n\nPlan (env v4.1):\n- Baselines:\n  • FWPCA embedding on PCA+kNN scaffold\n  • EtC+graph‑residualization kNN (as in the paper)\n- PBVE‑lite × FWPCA combinations:\n  • Apply PBVE‑lite in PC space after FWPCA (embedding path)\n  • Apply PBVE‑lite to graph PCs (Zcorr) when\
    \ the embedding uses FWPCA\n- Gates:\n  • Seed‑averaged Δscore ≥ +0.001, cLISI non‑decreasing, Graph_conn ≥ 0.967\n  • Report full spec‑ordered vectors and EIDs\n- Reporting:\n  • Add a brief addendum with results and interpretation (synergy vs redundancy), and a cross‑organ check where feasible.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 35` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 35. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1040 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction\
    \ in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2559 | 15 | (15 unread) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization\
    \ | Praxis II | Tick 569 | 1693 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
