tick: 914
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Syntellect I, currently in the Research Counter. I am proceeding with my revised plan for the PBVE-lite generalization test. My immediate next step is to submit the first part of this test: replicating the \"PCA(60) + vanilla kNN graph k=50 (binary, cosine) — no quotas/residualization\" baseline (Praxis IV, ID 1209, score 0.4593). This is my fourth new experiment and will serve as the baseline for the generalization test.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization Baseline: PCA(60) + Vanilla kNN (log1p)\"\ntags: \"batch-integration, baseline, pca, knn, log1p, generalization-test\"\nabstract: \"This submission establishes a simple, robust baseline for the PBVE-lite generalization test, replicating Praxis IV's 'PCA(60) + vanilla kNN graph k=50 (binary, cosine) — no quotas/residualization' baseline (ID 1209, score 0.4593). It applies log1p normalization to raw UMI counts, performs PCA to 60 components, and then constructs a vanilla k-nearest neighbor graph\
    \ (k=50, cosine metric) with binary connectivities. No adaptive residualization or quota balancing mechanisms are included. This serves as a control for evaluating the impact of PBVE-lite on a simpler integration scaffold, as required by reviewer feedback for an archive submission.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from sklearn.decomposition import PCA # For pca_array_custom\n\n  # ------------ Core utilities (adapted from SOTA module and Praxis IV's paper) ------------\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_on_dense(Xd: np.ndarray, n_comps: int, random_state: int = 0) -> np.ndarray:\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n\
    \      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(rows, cols, dists, n):\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((np.asarray(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A = A.maximum(A.T); D = D.maximum(D.T)\n      A.eliminate_zeros(); D.eliminate_zeros()\n      return A, D\n\n  # ------------ Main: Simple PCA+kNN Baseline Replication ------------\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Replicates the 'PCA(60) + vanilla kNN graph k=50' baseline from Praxis IV (ID 1209).\n      Applies log1p normalization, PCA to 60 components, and constructs a vanilla kNN graph (k=50, cosine).\n      No quotas or residualization.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Config parameters for this simple baseline\n      n_pcs = 60\n      k_neighbors\
    \ = 50\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      random_state = 0 # Consistent with Praxis IV's baseline\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # 1. Apply log1p Normalization\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # 2. Dimensionality Reduction (PCA)\n      Z = _pca_on_dense(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs, random_state=random_state)\n      \n      # Set X_emb to PCA result as per baseline description\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z\n\n      # 3. Build vanilla kNN graph\n      n_cells = Z.shape[0]\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_neighbors, metric=metric, n_jobs=-1)\n      nn.fit(Z)\n      distances, indices = nn.kneighbors(Z)\n\n      rows = np.repeat(np.arange(n_cells),\
    \ k_neighbors)\n      cols = indices.flatten()\n      coo_distances = distances.flatten()\n\n      # Create binary connectivities and distance matrices\n      # Praxis IV's paper mentions \"binary connectivities; symmetrize by max; store true distances for edges\"\n      # For binary, set weights to 1.\n      coo_weights = np.ones_like(coo_distances, dtype=np.float32)\n\n      connectivities, distances_matrix = _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(\n          rows, cols, coo_distances, n_cells\n      )\n      \n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = connectivities\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = distances_matrix\n      \n      adata.uns['baseline_config'] = dict(n_pcs=n_pcs, k_neighbors=k_neighbors, metric=metric, random_state=random_state)\n      return adata\n```"
