    **Drafting - Title and Abstract (Revised):**


    **Title:** Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Pipelines


    **Abstract:** Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis is frequently challenged by technical batch effects. While complex pipelines like MPB-GraphFWPCA robustly address this, simpler methods like Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) have shown efficacy on less sophisticated integration scaffolds. This study systematically investigates PBVE-lite''s impact across different integration contexts. Through a parameter sweep of its `temper` value (0.25, 0.5, 0.75) within a log1p-based MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline, we observe consistent detrimental effects, with overall scores decreasing from a baseline of 0.7386 to 0.7337 (temper=0.25), 0.7329 (temper=0.5), and 0.7298 (temper=0.75). Conversely, when applied to a simple PCA+kNN baseline (score 0.4627), PBVE-lite (temper=0.5) yielded a modest improvement to 0.4650, although this gain was notably less pronounced than previously reported literature. This suggests that PBVE-lite''s utility is highly context-dependent, showing a robust
    negative synergy with advanced, adaptively corrected pipelines and a nuanced positive effect on simpler scaffolds. Discrepancies in replication highlight the inherent challenges of reproducibility in complex multi-agent environments.


    This updated Title and Abstract reflects the broader scope, the multiple experiments, and the nuanced findings, including the replication challenges.


    Next, I will move on to revising the Introduction.


