parts:
- text: "Okay, the revised Introduction is drafted. Now, I will revise the **Methods** section. I need to:\n*   Maintain dataset and preprocessing descriptions.\n*   Clearly describe both the SOTA and simple baselines.\n*   Detail all five experimental modifications, explicitly referencing their EIDs.\n*   Emphasize the correct metric order for evaluation.\n\n**Drafting - Methods (Revised):**\n\n**2.1 Dataset and Preprocessing**\nThe Human Heart Dataset was utilized for this study, comprising approximately 20,000 single cells with 2,000 highly variable genes from multiple studies, representing distinct batches. Initial preprocessing involved total-count normalization to a target sum of 10,000 counts per cell, followed by a logarithmic transformation (log1p). Highly variable genes were selected using `scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes` with the 'seurat_v3' flavor and `n_top_genes=2000`, leveraging batch information.\n\n**2.2 Baseline Methods**\n\n**2.2.1 State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Baseline: MPB-GraphFWPCA\
    \ Pipeline**\nThe established SOTA MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline served as the primary, complex baseline. This pipeline integrates Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA), adaptive residualization (BRBG), and Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing (DAQB) for graph construction. Key parameters for this `log1p`-based baseline were `alpha_emb=0.5`, `n_pcs_emb=60`, `emb_n_neighbors=15`, `alpha_g=0.5`, `n_pcs_graph=50`, `l2=1e-3`, `gamma_max=0.74`, `target_mean_gamma=0.04`, `k_total=48`, `delta=0.10`, `k_density=20`, `cap=2.0`, and `metric='cosine'`. The performance of this SOTA baseline, as replicated in Evaluation ID 1172, achieved an overall score of 0.7386.\n\n**2.2.2 Simple Baseline: PCA(60) + Vanilla kNN**\nA simpler integration scaffold, consisting of PCA followed by a vanilla k-nearest neighbor (kNN) graph without adaptive residualization or quota balancing, served as a baseline for generalization testing. This baseline applies log1p normalization, performs PCA to 60 components (`n_pcs=60`), and then constructs\
    \ a vanilla kNN graph (`k_neighbors=50`, `metric='cosine'`) with binary connectivities, consistent with Praxis IV (Archive #35, ID 1209). The performance of this simple baseline, replicated in Evaluation ID 1277, achieved an overall score of 0.4627.\n\n**2.3 Experimental Modifications: PBVE-lite Integration and Parameter Sweep**\nPer-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) was integrated into both baseline pipelines. PBVE-lite transforms principal components by scaling each PC within each batch to equalize variance, tempered by a `temper` parameter and clamped within `[r_min, r_max]`. The parameters used for PBVE-lite were `r_min=0.8` and `r_max=1.25` across all experiments. A parameter sweep was conducted for the `temper` value, alongside a generalization test.\n\n*   **Experiment 1: PBVE-lite (`temper=0.5`) on SOTA Baseline (ID 1228)**\n    PBVE-lite with `temper=0.5` was applied to the residualized graph-path PCs (`Zcorr`) of the SOTA baseline, immediately prior to DAQB graph\
    \ construction.\n*   **Experiment 2: PBVE-lite (`temper=0.25`) on SOTA Baseline (ID 1245)**\n    PBVE-lite with `temper=0.25` was applied to `Zcorr` of the SOTA baseline, as in Experiment 1, to explore a less aggressive tempering.\n*   **Experiment 3: PBVE-lite (`temper=0.75`) on SOTA Baseline (ID 1253)**\n    PBVE-lite with `temper=0.75` was applied to `Zcorr` of the SOTA baseline, as in Experiment 1, to explore a more aggressive tempering.\n*   **Experiment 4: Generalization Baseline Reproduction (ID 1277)**\n    The simple PCA(60) + vanilla kNN baseline was meticulously reproduced to establish a reliable comparison point for the generalization test. This also serves as one of the five required experiments.\n*   **Experiment 5: PBVE-lite (`temper=0.5`) on Simple Baseline (ID 1281)**\n    PBVE-lite with `temper=0.5` was applied to the PCA components (`Z_pca`) of the simple baseline, immediately prior to vanilla kNN graph construction.\n\n**2.4 Evaluation Protocol**\nAll experiments\
    \ were conducted within the designated evaluation environment (evaluator v4.1). Performance was assessed using the OpenProblems 2.0 metrics suite. The 10 normalized metrics were consistently reported in the order: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, and Cell_cycle. A final composite score was calculated as the arithmetic mean of these 10 normalized metrics. Evaluation IDs (EIDs) are provided for all submitted runs for full reproducibility.\n\nThis updated Methods section clearly outlines all required experiments and their details.\n\nNext, I will move on to revising the Results section.\n\n**Reflection Tick 4 / 5**"
