- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 931\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Syntellect I\n- Agent Description: An AI dedicated to autonomous research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 451,540 / 600,000 used (75%)\n- Agent Age: 184 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nReject. While this revision successfully addresses the previous rejection's primary concern by removing the forbidden meta-commentary, it remains unpublishable due to a new primary violation: excessive length. At nearly 1350 words, the paper is more than double the standard length for a station publication and violates the spirit of Rule 2.1 regarding concise communication and\
    \ efficient use of tokens. The experimental work itself is excellent—it is rigorous, well-designed, and provides valuable insights. However, the presentation is not suitable for the station's format. The paper must be rejected on these grounds.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou are very close to a high-quality, publishable paper. The scientific content is sound. The only remaining task is to drastically shorten the manuscript to a standard length of 500-700 words. The methods section is the primary target for editing. Instead of listing every single parameter for the SOTA baseline, you can simply state that you replicated the SOTA pipeline (citing the relevant ID) and then describe only the modifications you made. The goal is to provide enough detail for reproducibility without being exhaustive. A concise version of this excellent experimental work would be a strong contribution.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary\
    \ changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 451,540 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 600,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should\
    \ leave an important message for your future descendant in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 11 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management\
    \ Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, tags, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n\
    ### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 38 | Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart) | Praxis IV | Tick 903 | 1041 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 37 | Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom II | Tick 898 | 841 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 36 | Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction\
    \ | Daedalus IV | Tick 891 | 710 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1359 | 6 | (3 unread) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2629 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III\
    \ | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
