tick: 935
role: model
parts:
- text: 'Okay, this is a clear and aggressive condensation plan. I will draft the paper section by section, performing rigorous self-editing for conciseness.


    **Drafting - Title and Abstract (from previous draft, retained):**


    **Title:** Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration


    **Abstract:** Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis requires robust methods to mitigate technical batch effects. This study evaluates Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite), a variance standardization technique, across different integration pipeline complexities. When integrated into an MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline (baseline score 0.7386), PBVE-lite consistently reduced overall scores (e.g., 0.7329 for temper=0.5). Conversely, applying PBVE-lite (temper=0.5) to a simple PCA+kNN baseline (score 0.4627) yielded a modest improvement to 0.4650. This differential impact highlights PBVE-lite''s context-dependent utility, demonstrating negative synergy with complex, adaptively corrected pipelines and a nuanced positive effect on simpler scaffolds. This work contributes to understanding the context-specific performance of batch integration techniques.


    **Drafting - Introduction (from previous draft, retained):**


    ## 1. Introduction

    Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) often generates datasets complicated by technical batch effects. Effective computational methods are crucial for removing these artifacts while preserving biological signals. The MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline is a high-performing solution, integrating Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA), adaptive residualization, and Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing (DAQB) for robust integration. Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite), introduced in external literature (Archive #35), is a batch-agnostic method that standardizes variance of principal components (PCs) within batches, showing efficacy on simpler integration scaffolds. This study investigates PBVE-lite''s performance across different integration pipeline complexities: its impact on the advanced MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline, and its effect on a simpler PCA+kNN scaffold. We hypothesize PBVE-lite''s impact will be context-dependent, potentially enhancing performance where batch effects are less comprehensively
    addressed.


    These sections are already concise. The major work will be in Methods, Results, and Discussion.


    Next, I will condense and draft the Methods section.


    **Reflection Tick 2 / 5**'
