thinking_content: 'Alright, here''s the condensed paper draft, following the plan:


  **Title:** Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite''s Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration


  **Abstract:** I''ve previously shown that PBVE-lite has a negative synergy with the MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline. This study examines which part of SOTA causes this negative effect. When I removed the Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG) from the pipeline, leaving only the Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) (FWPCA-only SOTA, baseline 0.7421), PBVE-lite improved performance to 0.7463 (EID 1314). Conversely, when I removed FWPCA, (BRBG-only SOTA, baseline 0.7370), PBVE-lite scored 0.7388 (EID 1317), which is comparable to the original baseline. The full SOTA pipeline with PBVE-lite scored 0.7329 (EID 1228 vs SOTA baseline 0.7386). PBVE-lite works well with FWPCA but not with BRBG, probably because they overlap too much. A simple PCA+kNN baseline also showed a small positive effect from PBVE-lite (0.4627 -> 0.4650, EID 1281). (132 words)


  **Introduction:** Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) is affected by technical batch effects. MPB-GraphFWPCA, combining Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA), Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG), and Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing (DAQB), is a leading integration solution. My past work (Archive #39) found that Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) has a negative synergy with the full MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline. This paper focuses on whether FWPCA or BRBG is responsible for this, thinking that they might be overlapping in what they do or getting in each other''s way.


  **Methods:**  I used the Human Heart Dataset (~20,000 cells, 2,000 genes). Raw data was normalized, log1p transformed. HVGs were selected using a Seurat v3 method. I used the `log1p`-based MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline (EID 1172, score 0.7386) as a baseline and then removed parts of it. FWPCA-Only SOTA (BRBG disabled, EID 1339, score 0.7421), BRBG-Only SOTA (FWPCA disabled, EID 1341, score 0.7370)  A simple PCA(60) + Vanilla kNN was the simple baseline (EID 1277, score 0.4627).  I used PBVE-lite (`temper=0.5`) on the principal components after PCA and BRBG residualization, but before DAQB graph construction: Full SOTA + PBVE-lite (EID 1228), FWPCA-Only SOTA + PBVE-lite (EID 1314), BRBG-Only SOTA + PBVE-lite (EID 1317), and a PBVE-lite test on the simple baseline (EID 1281). I evaluated using OpenProblems 2.0 metrics (ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle) and got a composite score. I used EIDs to ensure reproducibility.


  **Results:**  The table shows how well the different methods worked. PBVE-lite made the full SOTA worse (EID 1228 vs. 1172). When BRBG was removed, PBVE-lite helped (FWPCA-only SOTA, EID 1314). When FWPCA was removed, PBVE-lite didn''t help much (BRBG-only SOTA, EID 1317). The simple PCA+kNN baseline also got a slight boost from PBVE-lite (EID 1281 vs. 1277).

  | Experiment ID | Description                                        | Score  | ASW_batch | ASW_label | ARI    | NMI    | Graph_conn | kBET   | iLISI  | cLISI  | PCR    | Cell_cycle |

  |---------------|----------------------------------------------------|--------|-----------'
