    \ no additional benefit. These findings confirm negative synergy is specific to **BRBG**. PBVE-lite's per-batch PC variance equalization likely becomes redundant or actively interferes with BRBG's adaptive residualization, potentially over-correcting or distorting the PC space.\n\n**4.2 Contextual Factors and Generalization**\nThe generalization test on a simple PCA+kNN baseline with PBVE-lite showed a modest positive effect (+0.0023). This reinforces PBVE-lite's utility in simpler contexts. However, the magnitude was less than external reports (Praxis IV, Archive #35, EID 1210). This nuance highlights that PBVE-lite's performance may be sensitive to subtle parameter interactions or unstated environmental factors even on basic scaffolds.\n\n**4.3 Conclusion and Future Research**\nPBVE-lite positively synergizes with FWPCA but negatively interacts with BRBG. Its utility is contingent on specific adaptive mechanisms present in the integration pipeline. Future research should explore:\n\
    1.  **Adaptive Integration:** Strategies to adaptively control PBVE-lite's application based on other adaptive components.\n2.  **Order of Operations:** Examining if alternative application stages mitigate negative interactions.\n3.  **Alternative Standardization:** Exploring other batch-agnostic variance standardization methods harmonious with BRBG.\n\nThis completes the final draft of the paper.\n\n**Word count check:**\n- Title: 19 words\n- Abstract: 115 words\n- Introduction: 100 words\n- Methods: 202 words (approx.)\n- Results (textual summary only, excluding table): 145 words (approx.)\n- Discussion: 201 words (approx.)\n- **Total (excluding table): approx. 782 words.**\n\nI will make one last pass to trim for the target 500-700 words. The Methods and Discussion sections still have some room.\n\n**Final pass - trimming for word count:**\n\n**Revised Abstract:**\nPer-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) showed negative synergy with the MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline. This\
    \ study investigates if this interaction is specific to Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) or Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG). Ablation experiments on a log1p-based SOTA baseline (0.7386) revealed: when BRBG was disabled (FWPCA-only SOTA), PBVE-lite improved performance to 0.7463 (EID 1314). When FWPCA was disabled (BRBG-only SOTA), PBVE-lite's score (0.7388, EID 1317) was comparable to baseline. This indicates PBVE-lite positively synergizes with FWPCA but negatively interacts specifically with BRBG, likely due to redundancy or interference. (104 words, -11 from original)\n\n**Revised Methods:**\n## 2. Methods\n**2.1 Dataset and Preprocessing**\nHuman Heart Dataset (~20,000 cells, 2,000 HVGs) used. Raw UMI counts normalized (10,000/cell), log1p transformed. HVGs selected (`seurat_v3`, `n_top_genes=2000`).\n\n**2.2 Baselines and Experiments**\n*   **SOTA Baseline:** `log1p`-based MPB-GraphFWPCA (EID 1172, score 0.7386). Full parameters in EID 1172.\n*   **Simple Baseline:** PCA(60) +\
